= Existing Investigations Into The Compactified Two-Channel Kondo Model

The standard two-channel Kondo model,
$ H_"2CK" = J_1 bold(upright(S_f)) dot bold(upright(sigma_1)) + J_2 bold(upright(S_f)) dot bold(upright(sigma_2)) + H_(c 1) + H_(c 2), $
has a stable intermediate coupling fixed point that evades a strong-coupling expansion using standard renormalised peturbation theory.  This motivates a mapping where one replaces the two spin currents on distinct channels with a spin and charge current on the same channel:
$ H_"2CK" = J_1 bold(upright(S_f)) dot bold(upright(sigma)) + J_2 bold(upright(S_f)) dot bold(upright(tau)) + H_(c) $
where $bold(upright(sigma))$ and $bold(upright(tau))$ are the spin and charge isospin on the first bath site. In particular, $bold(upright(tau)) = sum_(alpha,beta) tilde(c)^dagger_alpha bold(upright(sigma)) tilde(c)_beta$, where $tilde(c)$ is the Nambu spinor:
$ tilde(c) = vec(c_arrow.t, c^dagger_arrow.b). $
This gives
$ bold(upright(tau))^plus.minus equiv 1/2 (bold(upright(tau))^x plus.minus i bold(upright(tau))^y) = c^dagger_arrow.t c^dagger_arrow.b thin \/ thin c_arrow.b c_arrow.t$. Because spin and charge cannot reside at the same time, such a model cannot overscreen the impurity, and this removes the intermediate-coupling fixed point, making the strong-coupling fixed point stable.
